<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · Maritime Southeast Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Portuguese Timor</h1><p class="st-meta">1702 CE – 1975 CE · capital Lifau (Dili from 1769)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Portugal's colony on the eastern half of Timor for nearly three centuries, whose sudden end in 1975 led to Indonesian invasion.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Dominican missionaries and traders from the Portuguese base on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solor">Solor</a> had been active on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timor">Timor</a> since the 16th century, buying sandalwood. In 1702 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antonio-coelho-guerreiro">António Coelho Guerreiro</a> arrived at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lifau">Lifau</a> as the first governor, but real power on the island was long contested by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/topasses">Topasses</a>, mixed-race Portuguese-speaking warlords.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Portugal ruled through alliances with local kings (liurai) and the Catholic Church. The capital moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dili">Dili</a> in 1769. Sandalwood gave way to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coffee-economy">coffee</a> in the 19th century, and borders with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> were settled in 1859 and 1914, leaving the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oecusse">Oecusse</a> enclave. Governor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jose-celestino-da-silva">José Celestino da Silva</a> extended control by force, and the Manufahi revolt of 1912 was crushed.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Occupied by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> from 1942 to 1945 at great cost to its people, the colony returned to Portugal. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carnation-revolution">Carnation Revolution</a> of 1974 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fretilin">Fretilin</a> declared independence in November 1975, but <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/indonesia">Indonesia</a> invaded in December. Independence finally came as <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/east-timor">East Timor</a> in 2002.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/portuguese-timor.jpg" alt="Portuguese Timor" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Original: Columbano Bordalo Pinheiro Vector: Vítor Luís Rodrigues, António Martins-Tuválkin · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Portugal_(official).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1702 CE – 1975 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Lifau (Dili from 1769)</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>António Coelho Guerreiro, José Celestino da Silva</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Roman Catholicism, traditional religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Portuguese, Tetum</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>East Timor</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Timor Portugues</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1838&amp;focus=portuguese-timor" aria-label="Portuguese Timor on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="8150 2497 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Portuguese Timor lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M8470 2753L4773 1044M8470 2753L8529 1145M8470 2753L7959 2664M8470 2753L8470 2753"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M4773 1044h0M8529 1145h0M7959 2664h0M8470 2753h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8470 2753h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M8470 2753h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1838&amp;focus=portuguese-timor">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
